 ## Overview
-Truncated Tensor Decomposition is a post-processing technique that reduces the multilinear rank of an already computed Tucker decomposition to achieve data compression or interactive level-of-detail (LOD) [S1, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of balancing reconstruction fidelity against storage and computational cost by explicitly selecting a reduced rank $(R'_1, R'_2, ..., R'_N)$ smaller than the original Tucker rank $(R_1, R_2, ..., R_N)$ [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Tensor Approximation](5362-tensor-approximation.md), which computes the decomposition from scratch to find the optimal rank, or [Tensor-Specific Quantization](6775-tensor-specific-quantization.md), which focuses on bit-depth reduction, Truncated Tensor Decomposition leverages the all-orthogonality property of the Tucker core to allow progressive, ex-post truncation without recomputing the full decomposition [S1, unofficial].
+Truncated Tensor Decomposition is a post-processing technique that reduces the multilinear rank of an already computed Tucker decomposition to achieve data compression or interactive level-of-detail (LOD) [S1, S3, unofficial]. It explicitly selects a reduced rank $(K_1, K_2, ..., K_N)$ smaller than the initial Tucker rank $(R_1, R_2, ..., R_N)$ to achieve compression [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Tensor Approximation](5362-tensor-approximation.md), which computes the decomposition from scratch, or [Tensor-Specific Quantization](6775-tensor-specific-quantization.md), which focuses on bit-depth reduction, Truncated Tensor Decomposition leverages the all-orthogonality property of the Tucker core to allow progressive, ex-post truncation without recomputing the full decomposition [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A full Tucker decomposition of an $N$-dimensional tensor $\mathcal{A}$, consisting of factor matrices $\mathbf{U}^{(n)} \in \mathbb{R}^{I_n \times R_n}$ for $n=1 \dots N$, and a core tensor $\mathcal{B} \in \mathbb{R}^{R_1 \times R_2 \times \dots \times R_N}$.
-*   **Input:** Target reduced ranks $K_n$ for each mode, where $1 \le K_n < R_n$.
-*   **Output:** A rank-reduced approximation $\tilde{\mathcal{A}}$ represented by truncated factor matrices $\mathbf{U}^{(n)}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{I_n \times K_n}$ and a truncated core tensor $\mathcal{B}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{K_1 \times K_2 \times \dots \times K_N}$.
